Z||000002||Voytek||10-26-2001||03:11 PM||||When you say 'coupe' - do you mean 4 cyl or V6? (like a Formula?)<P>Oil pan gasket - a fairly simple procedure.  I've done one on a V6 - the only problem was that I had to remove an engine mount.  Just be sure the engine is supported adequately.  I'm not sure about the 4 cyl.<P>Valve cover gasket - I will assume that you DO have the 4-cyl since you say gaskET.  This is a lot simpler on the 4-cyl than on the V6.<P>You'll only need some common sense for both.  Getting a Haynes or Chilton manual might be helpful.<P>One thing to remember:  do not tighted the pan/valve cover bolts too much.  Over-tightening will warp the gasket(s) and the engine will still leak.  The books will tell you the torque.  If you don't have a torque wrench or books, just remember: don't reef on the wrench too much - a little over snug is good enough in most cases.<P>One other thing.  Some people / shops / mechanics will tell you that you need the 'make-a'gasket' kind of stuff to use in conjunction with the gaskets.  You do NOT need this stuff if you are using rubber gaskets.  I think the only time you would use it is with cork gaskets.<P>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Pheagey||10-27-2001||11:47 AM||||Yea I have to 4 banger. I am told, and have verified, that the oil filter is indie the oil pan. Am I wrong in assuming thew oil pan has to be removed to replace the filter? I was planning to change the filter and the oil pan gasket at the same time then shortly later change the valve cover. I'm assuming both do not requrem more than a manual (got one), and a torque wrench (getting one).<P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Pheagey Grean<BR>"The time to talk is over. The time for diplomacy is over, the time for bluffing is over. The time to kick some f!@king as$ is now."<P>'88 coupe 'Hotwheels'<P>Http://www.LithiumEffects.com/car.html<P>Kills: '97 Civic LX Auto,<BR>Killed by: -||||reg||7
Z||000004||malacite||10-28-2001||09:32 PM||||i just installed a rocker arm cover gasket on my '88. it took a couple hours and about 40 bucks. i bought a torque wrench though. if you already have one of those it'll be cheaper.<P>new gasket...$8.00 (discount auto)<BR>rtv sealent...$3.00 (also discount)<BR>high temp engine paint...$3.00 (not nessisary, but since i had it off anyway)<BR>gasket scraper ...$2.00<BR>elbow grease...(i can't find this stuff anywhere)||||reg||1
Z||000005||Rodrv6||10-28-2001||10:22 PM||||You don't have to take the pan off to change the internal oil filter.  There is a large plug in the bottom of the pan.  Unscrew it and the filter can be pulled out through the hole.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
